\details{
In the left panel, the line is the BH linear step-up threshold,
\code{p_(i) = (i / m) * q}; in the right panel, it is the adaptive BKY
threshold, \code{p_(i) = (i / m) * q_star}, which uses \code{q_star} -- rescaled
from \code{q} using the estimated proportion of true nulls, \code{pi0_hat = m0_hat / m} -- rather than a fixed \code{q}. Points below their panel's
Every rank at or before the final cutoff is drawn in blue (rejected),
whereas ranks after it are drawn in grey (not rejected). The
dashed vertical line marks the cutoff rank \code{k}: the step-up rule
rejects every hypothesis from rank 1 up to \code{k}, not just the
individual points that happen to fall under the line -- \code{k} is the
\emph{last} point (in increasing p order) still below the threshold, and
everything at or before it is rejected even if a point in between sits
slightly above the line by chance. Because BKY's threshold adapts to
\code{pi0_hat}, it typically sits above BH's fixed-slope line whenever
\code{pi0_hat < 1} -- i.e. whenever some cells are estimated to have a real
trend -- which is why the right panel usually shows more rejections
